(;GM[1]FF[4]CA[UTF-8]AP[otools-weiqi]SZ[19]KM[0]PB[Shi Xiang-Xia 施襄夏]PW[Fan Xi-Ping 范西屏]DT[1739]PC[Danghu (Pinghu), Zhejiang]GN[当湖十局 第五局]RE[W+29]DTX[自公开记录整理：Jim Yu (JZY) 1992 年发布于 rec.games.go，Smart Go 格式转 SGF]AB[dp][pd]AW[dd][pp]C[Game 5

White: Fan Xi-Ping

Black: Shi Xiang-Xia];W[qf];B[qk];W[qm];B[nq];W[iq];B[mc];W[cn];B[ci];W[cg];B[fc];W[kc];B[gq];W[qi];B[no]C[Now R9 becomes weaker, so B O5 to strengthen O3 on the other side.  O5 can be also played at L3.];W[ck]C[Same principle: after B O5, W J3 becomes weaker, so W C9 to strengthen C6 on the other side.];B[ic]C[Again, after W C9, B F17 becomes weaker, so B J17 to strengthen F17.  All O5, C9, and J17 are examples of avoiding weak stones on "two sides."];W[on]C[The efficient way to attack R9 is to press O3 and O5.];B[nn];W[oq];B[or];W[pr];B[nr];W[nm]C[Today's players may choose P7, but Shi and Fan thought P7 would be "slow." This is again the different styles between now and then.];B[mm];W[nl];B[ml];W[mk]C[Strong move.  Shi explained: if W plays O9 instead, B would have J5 and B would be better \(W corner is not clean\).  W O9, Shi continued, would be correct only when R11 is at P14.];B[qr]C[To exchange with W Q1.  Good order.];W[ps];B[nk]C[Seems to be casual.  Q5 would be sharp.];W[oo]C[Clever.];B[np]C[Since W P5 created a cutting point at N6, B has to connect at O4.  With this exchange, B's future activity at the corner has been limited.];W[ok];B[nj];W[oj]C[W P9 and P10 look mediocre, but W really can't go with other choices.  Q7 \(instead of O9\) seems to be a move to defend here, but the result isn't quite clean -- B has S6 to live the corner.];B[qn]C[Slightly late.];W[rn]C[Now if B plays R5, W S5 is good and B can't live here.];B[ri]C[W has to be careful here.  If W casually plays S12, B has Q11 sente.  After W R12 and B O11, W has to play M9 and B would have a good move at Q7.  That would be a big headache to W.];W[qh]C[Calm.  Now look back, B had better saved S11.];B[op];W[pq];B[po];W[om];B[ro];C[The corner is not settled.  However, if B plays the corner now, although he could create a ko or live with seki, he doesn't have ko threats or, with seki, he would lose sente.  So a better choice for B is to play somewhere else.]W[rm];B[ni];W[lk];C[Urgent.]B[oi];C[Let B to save a few stones \(R9 etc.\).  Smart choice.  If W plays Q11 instead, B now can live the corner with seki *without* losing sente -- B would have Q8 \(now Q8 is not a threat because of W R4\).]W[qp];C[To here, W gets 20 plus points at the lower right.  B gains some outside potential, but this potential has been damaged by W M9.  Plus the faulty B S11, it can be judged that W is leading.]B[pj];W[of];B[pg];W[qg];B[li];W[ki];B[kh];C[W L11 then M7, good order.]W[lm];C[Should N6 directly, saving moves at L6 and L7.]B[ll];W[kl];B[mn];W[kj];B[jh];C[Good point.  Not only completely destroies B's potential, but also threatens B's lower right team.]W[ko];B[kq];W[kr]C[If W plays this move directly at J5, it's already W's advantage.  L2 is tight, leaving little breathing room for B.

Editor: L2 is not just good; it's deep.  Chen 9p used nine variation diagrams to explain why L2 is good.  Unfortunatly, I am unable to include all these variation here since gasoline today is so expensive. :\) Hope I *will* be able to include them when I re-edit these comments.];B[jk];W[kk];B[jp];C[Good move related to L2.]W[io];B[ip];W[lq];B[lp];W[hp];C[No choice.  If H3 cut, B's lower right team would be in danger.]B[kp];W[hq];C[Locally speaking, B H5 is better at K7.  But because of previous K4 and J4, B's lower left corner is hurt.  So if B wants to win, H5 is probably the only choice.]B[ho];C[W uses the most severe moves, obtains the best result.  Fan's \(playing W\) great skills are shown here.]W[hn];B[go];W[gp];B[in];W[jo];B[jm];W[im];B[jn];W[lo];C[If K8 instead, W would capture L4 five B stones.  Although B then is able to get W's middle stones, B couldn't turn the game around.]B[lr];W[fo];B[gn];W[hm];B[fn];W[jl];B[eo];W[fp];B[gl];W[jr];B[mq];C[W's lead is comfortable.]W[hk];B[gk];W[gm];B[en];W[fm];C[B is very persistent, fighting hard.  However, W's charge is so strong that B can do nothing but trying to keep up the pace.]B[dl];W[em];B[dm];W[dn];B[cq];W[dk];B[el];W[ek];B[fl];W[bm];B[hj];W[ik];B[eq];W[fq];B[er];W[fr];B[gj];W[ii];B[ei];W[bp];B[bq];W[cp];C[Damage W's eyeshape, but B's own corner is not clean yet.]B[do];W[br];B[ar];W[cr];B[dr];C[It seems that W can play B1 to create a ko, but it's a ko that W needs two moves to win.]W[bi];C[B must play B10, otherwise it would be like saying "resign."]B[bj];C[The following moves of both sides are tight and well calculated.]W[di];B[ch];W[dh];B[bk];W[bl];B[bg];C[Technique to make D10 sente.]W[bh];B[cj];W[dj];B[ah];W[bf];B[ai];W[bh];B[bi];W[cl];B[cf];W[dg];B[ce];C[Key point.  If F12 instead, since J10 is sente for B, B can play H12 and W's weakness would be exposed.]W[hh];B[gh];W[gg];C[B is persistent.]B[hg];W[fh];B[gi];W[hf];B[ig];W[ij];B[fg];W[gf];B[ff];W[fe];B[eh];W[ee];B[df];W[ef];B[eg];W[gd];B[co];W[bo];C[B has to live the corner.]B[bs];C[If A4, W is also winning, but globally speaking, K15 is clearly more important.]W[je];B[ap];W[ao];B[bn];W[aq];B[cm];W[dq];C[To here, B is advantageous locally...]B[an];C[...but W gains K15 and now L14 two key points, thus simplifed the situation.  Correct policy.]W[kf];B[ng];W[ne];C[Last struggle.]B[me];W[mf];B[nf];W[oe];B[lf];W[le];B[mg];C[W is flawless.]W[md];B[cd];W[mf];B[qo];W[rp];B[me];W[cn];B[dn];W[mf];B[pl];W[pm];B[me];W[am];B[cn];W[mf];B[ol];C[Winning ko with sente.  Victory is unshakable.]W[lg];B[lh];W[cc];B[kb];W[bc];B[be];W[jb];B[lb];W[jc];B[pc];C[W continues to fight even though he has won for sure.]W[rd];B[rc];C[Good technique.]W[rb];B[qc];W[sc];B[re];W[rf];B[qd];C[W is confident to win this ko.]W[se];B[sd];W[og];B[nh];W[rd];B[hl];W[il];B[sd];W[mj];B[mi];W[rd];B[ln];W[kn];B[sd];W[ej];B[fi];W[rd];B[ji];W[km];B[sd];W[mo];B[mp];W[rd];B[sp];W[pn];B[sd];W[ql];B[pk];W[rd];B[so];W[sq];B[sd];W[os];B[ms];W[rd];B[jj];W[jn];B[sd];W[nc];B[nb];W[rd];B[if];C[W ignores B J14 -- let B "come in" but R15 kills B upper right. W's winning margin is now bigger.]W[qe];B[ie];W[lc];B[ib];W[ja];B[eb];W[db];B[ec];W[dc];C[If B doesn't protect, W H17 can cut down three B stones.]B[hd];W[ad];B[bd];W[ac];B[ae];W[ba];B[cq];W[cs];B[rk];W[ge];B[rh];W[gb];B[gc];C[White wins by 14.5 zi \(29 pts\).

Overview: B had some loss in the lower right corner fight.  With the resulting advantage, W frequently attacked B with tight and clever moves.  Although B fought very hard, there was no chance -- W's calculation was flawless, good moves were endless, and his attacks were breathless.  Fan's masterpiece.]W[ia])
